  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_FontData:
    m_Font: {fileID: 12800000, guid: 5a8ef543591268943a212da7372c3294, type: 3}
    m_FontSize: 75
    m_FontStyle: 0
    m_BestFit: 0
    m_MinSize: 0
    m_MaxSize: 186
    m_Alignment: 4
    m_AlignByGeometry: 0
    m_RichText: 1
    m_HorizontalOverflow: 0
    m_VerticalOverflow: 0
    m_LineSpacing: 1
  m_Text: Mangrove Forests make up one of the most plentiful plant populations in
    the Everglade Ecosystem. They are present near the coastal channels and winding
    rivers around the tip of South Florida. The term "mangrove" does not refer to
    a particular botanical relation, but rather is used to identify several species
    of salt-tolerant trees that thrive amidst the harsh growing conditions of the
    coast. These come in three varieties; Red mangroves (Rhizophora mangle), known
    for their stilt-like roots, Black mangroves (Aveciennia germinans) and white
    mangroves (Laguncularia racemosa) which thrive within tidal waters, where freshwater
    from the Everglades mixes with saltwater. The Everglades boasts the largest contigious
    stand of protected mangrove forest in the Western Hemisphere.
